From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 15:00:00 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] Patched editions: a modded disc on the release's recompiled
 code

A mod that patches a game's disc changes a little of its code: a few
hundred words of the executable and of its overlays, and code it loads
and writes into the executable while it runs. Recompiling the whole
game for every version of such a mod is what made mods impractical.

A patched edition keeps the release's recompiled code instead. The
disc's own executable (a plain ELF; an encrypted one cannot be patched)
is loaded over the release's, and every generated unit holding a word
that differs from the release is taken out of dispatch, so that the
interpreter runs it from guest memory. A whole unit, because calls
inside a unit are direct jumps the runtime never sees; chains into it
already fall back to the exact per-address lookup, which finds nothing
and returns to dispatch. An overlay no corpus matches is compared with
the release's overlays, read from the release's disc and matched by the
corpus's own hash, and runs on the closest corpus the same way. Code the
game writes later is found when it flushes a cache: the executable is
compared again on an instruction cache flush, and on a data cache flush
of a range of it.

- Runtime::retire_recompiled_unit(): takes a unit out of dispatch,
  keeping host replacements; registered_function() still answers for
  what it took, so a replacement can still call the function it stands
  in for.
- host/patched_edition: reading a patched disc (its executable, and its
  PARAM.SFO's MEMSIZE for 64 MiB of memory), the comparisons, and the
  release's overlays.
- GameProfile::patched_disc: the port says which disc to play;
  <prefix>_PATCHED_DISC=<image> for a run, <prefix>_NO_PATCHED_DISC=1
  to ignore both. Its saves and downloads stay apart, in <ms0>-patched,
  unless <prefix>_MEMORY_STICK_DIR names a memory stick.
- patched_overlay_hash(): for a port that tells its overlays apart by
  their corpus's hash.
- ui::request_menu_close(): for a port's row that opens a panel over the
  game, which only shows once the menu is closed; the menu then leaves
  the input to that panel instead of handing it back to the game.
- host/disc_patch: a mod's patch to the disc (VCDIFF, as xdelta3 writes
  it, with its LZMA secondary compression; the LZMA2 and xz decoding
  written from the public format descriptions) applied once to the
  release's image, keeping only the sectors it changes in a layer file.
  IsoImage opens a layer file as the patched image, its other sectors
  read from the release's, so nothing that reads the disc needs to know.

Verified with Dissidia 012 (ULUS10566) and the Emendatus mod on Windows,
clang build: the executable's 203 changed words take 7 of 119 units,
the menu overlay's 190 words 12 units, the battle overlay's 768 words 15
units, and 23 more units are taken when the mod writes code at run time
(16 sites). The title demo's battle runs at 1.2 ms of guest time a frame
(1.1 ms on the release); Story Mode's prologue scene plays; the mod's
saves load. The release starts as before, with no unit taken. The Emendatus
patch (11 MB) prepares in about 3 seconds into a 26 MB layer, whose
image is byte for byte the image xdelta3's format gives; the game plays
from it as from the patched image. Not
verified: a player-controlled battle, the framework's own build and
tests, Linux, Android, another game or mod.
